WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:04.399
IT used to have two camps: developers built software,

2
00:00:04.562 --> 00:00:06.501
operators kept the systems running.

3
00:00:07.562 --> 00:00:10.836
One side wanted fast change, the other calm.

4
00:00:11.555 --> 00:00:13.366
Code was thrown over the wall.

5
00:00:14.357 --> 00:00:17.852
DevOps removes that wall: one team builds and runs,

6
00:00:18.026 --> 00:00:20.313
and automates everything that recurs.

7
00:00:21.325 --> 00:00:24.239
The word appeared around two thousand and nine,

8
00:00:24.378 --> 00:00:28.418
when Patrick Debois organised the first DevOpsDays in Ghent.

9
00:00:29.757 --> 00:00:33.321
The heart of DevOps is the pipeline: an automated conveyor,

10
00:00:33.472 --> 00:00:36.921
the same steps for every change, from developer to user.

11
00:00:37.946 --> 00:00:42.881
It starts with version control, usually Git: every version of the code kept,

12
00:00:43.113 --> 00:00:44.344
and who changed what.

13
00:00:45.419 --> 00:00:50.376
Saving a change is a commit: a small package of changes with a short explanation.

14
00:00:51.695 --> 00:00:54.423
After a commit the pipeline starts itself.

15
00:00:55.123 --> 00:00:59.024
Step one, the build: the code becomes something a computer can run.

16
00:01:00.035 --> 00:01:03.994
Step two: automated tests check that everything still works.

17
00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:08.100
Step three: the result is stored as an artefact,

18
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.794
a fixed package that no longer changes.

19
00:01:11.851 --> 00:01:16.854
Step four: installing on a test environment, and later on the real systems.

20
00:01:17.868 --> 00:01:23.301
If a step fails, like a red test, the conveyor stops and the team is told at once.

21
00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:29.534
For a Lean consultant, familiar: a pipeline works like standard work for software,

22
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:31.996
the same route, a predictable outcome.

23
00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:34.843
Stopping at a fault is jidoka,

24
00:01:35.064 --> 00:01:39.058
the Toyota idea that a machine stops as soon as something goes wrong:

25
00:01:39.185 --> 00:01:40.509
no faults passed on.

26
00:01:41.502 --> 00:01:46.274
Small commits are like small batches: less work in between, faults seen sooner.

27
00:01:47.304 --> 00:01:50.544
In proposals this is often called CI/CD:

28
00:01:50.671 --> 00:01:53.829
continuous integration and continuous delivery.

29
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:55.556
Both come later.

30
00:01:56.548 --> 00:02:00.867
Factories say: we are no software company, DevOps is not for us.

31
00:02:01.587 --> 00:02:06.661
Yet the MES, label software and ERP links change ever more often.

32
00:02:07.701 --> 00:02:12.020
Ask: how does a change reach the floor today, and which steps are manual?

33
00:02:12.705 --> 00:02:15.422
Each manual step means errors and waiting.

34
00:02:16.746 --> 00:02:17.559
An example:

35
00:02:17.675 --> 00:02:22.052
a dairy plant has its supplier make the best-before date on the label larger.

36
00:02:23.074 --> 00:02:26.058
At nine o'clock the commit: larger date on label.

37
00:02:26.781 --> 00:02:30.833
A minute later the pipeline starts; the build takes three minutes.

38
00:02:31.841 --> 00:02:33.791
At four past nine the tests,

39
00:02:33.988 --> 00:02:38.365
eight minutes, checking among other things that the date format is still right.

40
00:02:39.474 --> 00:02:43.735
At twelve past nine artefact four point seven point one is stored;

41
00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:48.460
at quarter past it is on the test environment; quality prints a test label.

42
00:02:49.433 --> 00:02:52.800
Total: fifteen minutes from commit to test environment,

43
00:02:52.963 --> 00:02:54.867
without a single manual step.

44
00:02:56.195 --> 00:02:57.681
How well does a team deliver?

45
00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:01.397
DORA measures it: DevOps Research and Assessment,

46
00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:05.775
surveying thousands of IT professionals yearly since twenty fourteen.

47
00:03:06.798 --> 00:03:07.785
Nicole Forsgren,

48
00:03:07.947 --> 00:03:12.370
Jez Humble and Gene Kim summed up the first years in the book Accelerate,

49
00:03:12.521 --> 00:03:16.201
twenty eighteen; late that year DORA joined Google Cloud.

50
00:03:16.967 --> 00:03:20.450
The State of DevOps report is known for a few metrics.

51
00:03:21.464 --> 00:03:22.613
Two are about speed.

52
00:03:23.335 --> 00:03:27.039
Deployment frequency: how often a new version goes live,

53
00:03:27.201 --> 00:03:29.465
from three times a day to once a month.

54
00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:34.258
Live means in production: the real systems that users use.

55
00:03:35.285 --> 00:03:37.828
Change lead time: from commit to production,

56
00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.336
the part of the chain the team controls.

57
00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:43.046
Two are about stability.

58
00:03:43.745 --> 00:03:48.505
Change fail rate: the share of deployments that need immediate intervention.

59
00:03:49.515 --> 00:03:52.441
For example a rollback, or an urgent fix.

60
00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.358
Failed deployment recovery time:

61
00:03:55.613 --> 00:03:58.457
how long recovery takes when a deployment goes wrong.

62
00:03:59.494 --> 00:04:03.894
Until twenty twenty-three it was called MTTR, mean time to restore.

63
00:04:04.721 --> 00:04:07.751
Now only a failure from the team's own change counts,

64
00:04:07.937 --> 00:04:10.050
not a power cut in the data centre.

65
00:04:11.073 --> 00:04:12.605
Together, the four keys.

66
00:04:13.323 --> 00:04:17.386
The best teams score well on all four: speed and stability go together,

67
00:04:17.549 --> 00:04:20.544
as small changes are easier to test and recover.

68
00:04:21.858 --> 00:04:25.631
The Lean view: lead time is like lead time in a value stream,

69
00:04:25.806 --> 00:04:30.868
change fail rate like first pass yield, the share right first time, but reversed.

70
00:04:31.894 --> 00:04:35.226
You hear: we deploy once a quarter, so less goes wrong.

71
00:04:35.972 --> 00:04:38.027
DORA often shows the reverse: rare,

72
00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.753
big deployments fail more often and recover more slowly.

73
00:04:42.737 --> 00:04:45.999
Ask an IT team: how often do you deploy to production,

74
00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:48.658
and how often must you step in straight after?

75
00:04:49.419 --> 00:04:51.973
If nobody knows, nothing is measured yet.

76
00:04:53.331 --> 00:04:58.869
An example: a confectionery plant's IT team builds ERP and MES links;

77
00:04:59.032 --> 00:05:00.843
you collect four weeks of numbers.

78
00:05:01.845 --> 00:05:05.978
Deployment frequency: eight deployments in four weeks, so two a week.

79
00:05:07.029 --> 00:05:10.663
Change lead time: six working days from commit to production,

80
00:05:10.825 --> 00:05:13.240
mainly waiting for a weekly test slot.

81
00:05:14.302 --> 00:05:18.911
Change fail rate: two of the eight rolled back at once, twenty-five percent.

82
00:05:19.679 --> 00:05:22.373
Recovered after one and after three hours.

83
00:05:23.384 --> 00:05:27.447
Conclusion: fairly frequent delivery, but one in four goes wrong.

84
00:05:28.219 --> 00:05:30.030
That is where to improve first.
